`useEffect(recheckUnsavedChanges)` runs on every commit and each run schedules a `setTimeout(..., 0)` that is never cleared. If the component unmounts (e.g. the screen is dismissed) with a timer still pending, the callback fires after unmount and calls `setShouldPreventRemove` on an unmounted component — an uncleaned resource / potential leak and a React state-update-after-unmount warning. A `setTimeout(…, 0)` scheduled on the final pre-unmount commit is not guaranteed to run before unmount.

Track the timer id and clear it in the effect's cleanup:

```tsx
const recheckUnsavedChanges = useCallback(() => {
    // return the id so the caller can clear it
}, [isFocused, getHasUnsavedChanges]);

useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => {
        setShouldPreventRemove(isFocused && !isSavingRef.current && getHasUnsavedChanges());
    }, 0);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
});
```

Note that the manual `recheckUnsavedChanges()` calls from the input change handlers also leak their timers and should be cleaned up (e.g. via a shared ref that is cleared on unmount).

This `Onyx.connectWithoutView` subscription is only ever disconnected on the success path (the `Onyx.disconnect(connection)` inside the `createdAgentEntry` branch). If `CREATE_AGENT` fails, the `failureData` restores the optimistic row with `pendingAction: ADD` and no real, server-created agent ever appears, so the `.find(...)` never matches, `resolve` is never called, and `Onyx.disconnect(connection)` is never reached. The subscription (and the pending Promise it lives in) then leaks for the lifetime of the app — and this repeats on every failed creation.

Add a cleanup path so the connection is always torn down. For example, subscribe to the request/failure state (or add a timeout) and disconnect there too:

```ts
function waitForCreatedAgentAccountID(optimisticAccountID: number): Promise<number> {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        let knownAgentAccountIDs: Set<number> | undefined;
        const connection = Onyx.connectWithoutView({
            key: ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.SHARED_NVP_AGENT_PROMPT,
            waitForCollectionCallback: true,
            callback: (agentPrompts) => {
                // ...existing logic...
                Onyx.disconnect(connection);
                resolve(realAccountID);
            },
        });

        // Ensure the subscription is always cleaned up (e.g. on CREATE_AGENT failure / timeout)
        // so it never leaks when the real agent never arrives.
        // e.g. watch failureData/pendingAction restore, or a bounded timeout, then:
        //   Onyx.disconnect(connection); reject(...);
    });
}
```

This `setTimeout` is created inside a `useEffect` but is never cleared on unmount. The effect body returns no cleanup function, so the scheduled callback can fire after the component has unmounted. The added comment explicitly notes that on small screens this page "detaches and unmounts" — which is precisely the scenario where a dangling timer leaks and runs against a torn-down component.

Capture the timer id and clear it in the effect's cleanup:

```tsx
if (policyCurrency === CONST.CURRENCY.USD && achData?.state === CONST.BANK_ACCOUNT.STATE.PENDING) {
    goToWithdrawalAccountSetupStep(CONST.BANK_ACCOUNT.STEP.VALIDATION);
    setShouldShowContinueSetupButton(shouldShowContinueSetupButtonValue);
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
        Navigation.navigate(ROUTES.BANK_ACCOUNT_USD_SETUP.getRoute({policyID: policyIDParam, page: CONST.BANK_ACCOUNT.PAGE_NAMES.VALIDATION, backTo}));
    }, 0);
    return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId);
}
```

`startWithLoading` schedules work via `setTimeout(resolve, 0)` but never stores or clears the timer. Because `runAfterPaint` typically triggers navigation/Onyx mutations that unmount this component, the deferred callback can fire after unmount — running `runAfterPaint()` and, on error, calling `setIsPressed(false)` on an unmounted component (state update on unmounted component / leaked timer). There is no cancellation tied to the component lifecycle.

Capture the timeout id and clear it on unmount, and guard the post-await work with a mounted/cancelled flag so it can't run after the component is gone:

```ts
const isMountedRef = useRef(true);
useEffect(() => () => { isMountedRef.current = false; }, []);

const startWithLoading = async (runAfterPaint: () => void) => {
    setIsPressed(true);
    await new Promise<void>((resolve) => { setTimeout(resolve, 0); });
    if (!isMountedRef.current) {
        return;
    }
    try {
        runAfterPaint();
    } catch (error) {
        if (isMountedRef.current) {
            setIsPressed(false);
        }
        throw error;
    }
};
```

`TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions` returns a `CancelHandle`, but the handle is not stored or cleaned up. If a transition is active when the component unmounts, the queued callback will still fire (calling `loadNewerChats` on an unmounted component).

Store the handle and return a cleanup function:
```tsx
const handle = TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions({
    callback: () => {
        requestAnimationFrame(() => loadNewerChats(false));
    },
});
return () => handle.cancel();
```

`TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions` returns a `CancelHandle`, but the handle is not stored or cleaned up in the effect. If a transition is active when the component unmounts or when deps change, the queued callback will still fire (calling `scrollToBottom` on an unmounted component).

Store the handle and return a cleanup function:
```tsx
const handle = TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions({
    callback: () => {
        reportScrollManager.scrollToBottom();
    },
});
return () => handle.cancel();
```

`TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions` returns a `CancelHandle`, but the handle is not stored or cleaned up in the effect. If a transition is active when the component unmounts or when deps change, the queued callback will still fire (calling `scrollToBottom` on an unmounted component).

Store the handle and return a cleanup function, similar to what is already done in the effect just above (line ~650):
```tsx
const handle = TransitionTracker.runAfterTransitions({
    callback: () => {
        reportScrollManager.scrollToBottom();
