The `setTimeout` inside the WebView `onLoadEnd` handler is not cleaned up. If the component unmounts (e.g., user dismisses the modal) before the 500ms timeout fires, `setCookiesCleared(true)` will be called on an unmounted component.

Consider storing the timeout ID in a ref and clearing it on unmount:

```tsx
const cookieTimerRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>();

// In onLoadEnd:
onLoadEnd={() => {
    cookieTimerRef.current = setTimeout(() => setCookiesCleared(true), CONST.MERGE_HR.COOKIE_CLEAR_DELAY_MS);
}}

// Add cleanup effect:
useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
        if (cookieTimerRef.current) {
            clearTimeout(cookieTimerRef.current);
        }
    };
}, []);
```

The `scheduleFollowUpScrolls` function creates chained `requestAnimationFrame` callbacks (up to `MEASURED_ANCHOR_SCROLL_FOLLOW_UP_FRAMES = 3` deep) that are not tracked or cancelled on cleanup. The cleanup effect on line 174 only cancels `runtime.pendingFrameId` but does not cancel the follow-up RAF chain. These callbacks could fire after the component unmounts, attempting to call `scrollToIndex` on a potentially stale or null ref.

Track follow-up frame IDs so they can be cancelled on cleanup. For example, store them in the runtime object:
```ts
type MeasuredScrollRuntime = {
    // ... existing fields
    followUpFrameIds: number[];
};

const scheduleFollowUpScrolls = (remainingFrames: number) => {
    if (remainingFrames <= 0) return;
    const id = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        tryApplyMeasuredAnchorScroll(layoutHeight, {isFollowUp: true});
        scheduleFollowUpScrolls(remainingFrames - 1);
    });
    runtime.followUpFrameIds.push(id);
};
```
Then cancel all follow-up frames in `cancelPendingMeasuredScrollFrame` and `resetMeasuredScrollRuntime`.

The `requestAnimationFrame` callback is not cancelled if the component unmounts before it fires. If the user navigates away (e.g., presses back) after `setIsSaving(true)` but before the rAF executes, the callback will still run and attempt `clearSelectedTransactions()` and `Navigation.dismissToPreviousRHP()` on an unmounted component, potentially causing stale operations or navigation glitches.

Store the rAF ID and cancel it on unmount, or cancel it in the existing cleanup effect:

```tsx
const rafIdRef = useRef<number>();

useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
        if (rafIdRef.current) {
            cancelAnimationFrame(rafIdRef.current);
        }
        clearBulkEditDraftTransaction();
    };
}, []);

// In save():
rafIdRef.current = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    // ... existing callback
});
```

The `requestAnimationFrame` call is not cancelled if the component unmounts before the callback fires. This means `updateMultipleMoneyRequests`, `clearSelectedTransactions`, and `Navigation.dismissToPreviousRHP()` could execute after the component has unmounted, causing side effects on stale state.

Store the rAF ID and cancel it on unmount, or use a ref to track whether the component is still mounted:

```tsx
const rafIdRef = useRef<number | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
        if (rafIdRef.current !== null) {
            cancelAnimationFrame(rafIdRef.current);
        }
    };
}, []);

// In save():
rafIdRef.current = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    // ... existing logic
    rafIdRef.current = null;
});
```

`useFocusEffect` internally uses `useEffect` with `[effect, navigation]` as dependencies. Passing an inline arrow function creates a new reference on every render, causing `search()` to fire on **every render** while the screen is focused -- not just on tab focus. This will result in a flood of API requests.

Wrap in `useCallback` with appropriate dependencies:

```ts
useFocusEffect(
    useCallback(() => {
        if (!queryJSON || hash === undefined || shouldUseLiveData || isOffline) {
            return;
        }
        if (isSnapshotDataLoaded || isSnapshotSearchLoading) {
            return;
        }
        search({queryJSON, searchKey: currentSearchKey, offset: 0, shouldCalculateTotals, isLoading: false, skipWaitForWrites: true});
    }, [hash, isOffline, shouldUseLiveData, queryJSON, isSnapshotDataLoaded, isSnapshotSearchLoading, currentSearchKey, shouldCalculateTotals]),
);
```

`useFocusEffect` internally uses `useEffect` with `[effect, navigation]` as dependencies. Passing an inline arrow function `() => { openSearch(); }` creates a new reference on every render, causing this effect to re-run on **every render** while the screen is focused. This means `openSearch()` will be called repeatedly instead of only on focus.

Wrap in `useCallback`:

```ts
useFocusEffect(
    useCallback(() => {
        openSearch();
    }, []),
);
```

`useFocusEffect` internally uses `useEffect` with `[effect, navigation]` as dependencies (see React Navigation source). Passing `clearOnHashChange` as an unstable plain function declaration means the internal effect tears down and re-subscribes focus/blur listeners on **every render** while the screen is focused. This was previously correct when `clearOnHashChange` was wrapped in `useCallback`.

Wrap the callback in `useCallback`:

```ts
const clearOnHashChange = useCallback(() => {
    if (hash === undefined) {
        return;
    }
    clearSelectedTransactions(hash);
}, [hash, clearSelectedTransactions]);
```

`firedActionIDs` is a module-level `Set` that grows unboundedly as users view goal celebrations across their session. It is never cleared or pruned, which constitutes a memory leak over long-lived sessions.

Either move this tracking into component state/ref so it is cleaned up on unmount, or add a cleanup mechanism (e.g., periodic pruning, or limit the Set size). If it must remain module-level, consider using a bounded data structure:

```tsx
// Option A: Use a ref inside the component
const firedActionIDsRef = useRef(new Set<string>());

// Option B: If module-level is needed, bound the size
const MAX_TRACKED_IDS = 200;
if (firedActionIDs.size > MAX_TRACKED_IDS) {
    const oldest = firedActionIDs.values().next().value;
    firedActionIDs.delete(oldest);
}
```

The nested `requestAnimationFrame` calls inside the `useEffect` lack cleanup. If the component unmounts after RAF 1 fires (setting `isInitialRender` to `false`) but before RAF 2 fires, `setHasLinkingSettled(true)` will execute against an unmounted component. While a single RAF is near-instant, the nested pattern doubles the window and introduces a real risk on slow devices or during fast navigation.

Return a cleanup function that cancels any pending animation frame:

```ts
useEffect(() => {
    if (!isInitialRender || !initialScrollKey) {
        return;
    }
    const outerFrame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        setIsInitialRender(false);
        innerFrameRef.current = requestAnimationFrame(() => setHasLinkingSettled(true));
    });
    return () => {
        cancelAnimationFrame(outerFrame);
        if (innerFrameRef.current != null) {
            cancelAnimationFrame(innerFrameRef.current);
        }
    };
}, [isInitialRender, initialScrollKey]);
```

Alternatively, use a ref to store the inner frame ID and cancel both in the cleanup.

The `setTimeout` inside `extendLoaderIfNeeded` has no cleanup mechanism. If the component unmounts before the timeout fires, `hideLoaderAndHandleNext` will call `setIsLoaderVisible(false)`, `setErrorQueue(...)`, `setFileError(...)`, `setIsErrorModalVisible(true)`, and potentially `reset()` on an unmounted component. Additionally, if the user triggers a new validation cycle before the timeout completes, the delayed callback will interfere with the new validation state.

Store the timeout ID in a ref and clear it in `reset()`:

```tsx
const loaderTimeoutRef = useRef<ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>>();

const reset = () => {
    if (loaderTimeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(loaderTimeoutRef.current);
        loaderTimeoutRef.current = undefined;
    }
    // ... existing reset logic
};

// In extendLoaderIfNeeded:
loaderTimeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
    hideLoaderAndHandleNext();
}, MIN_LOADER_VISIBLE_DURATION_MS - elapsedTime);
```

The `useEffect` at line 63 opens a bank connection popup window via `openBankConnection(url)` but has no cleanup function. If the component unmounts for any reason other than the user pressing the back button (e.g., navigation, route change), the popup window will remain open as a leaked resource.

Add a cleanup function to close the window on unmount:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (!url || isOffline) {
        return;
    }
    customWindow = openBankConnection(url);
    return () => {
        customWindow?.close();
    };
}, [url, isOffline]);
```

The `setTimeout` inside the `Promise.race` is never cleared in the effect's cleanup function. When the effect re-runs (e.g., `conciergeReportID` changes) or the component unmounts, the cleanup sets `cancelled = true` and removes the linking listener but does not call `clearTimeout`. The orphaned timer will still fire and resolve the racing promise, which is harmless due to the `cancelled` guard, but the timer itself is a leaked resource. If dependencies change rapidly, multiple orphaned timers accumulate.

Capture the timer ID and clear it in cleanup:

```tsx
let timeoutId: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout>;

Promise.race([
    Linking.getInitialURL(),
    new Promise<null>((resolve) => {
        timeoutId = setTimeout(() => resolve(null), CONST.TIMING.GET_INITIAL_URL_TIMEOUT);
    }),
])
    .then((url) => {
        // ...
    })
    .catch(() => {
        // ...
    });

// In cleanup:
return () => {
    cancelled = true;
    clearTimeout(timeoutId);
    linkingChangeListener.current?.remove();
};
```

