The `setTimeout` inside `useMemo` creates a timer that is never cleaned up. Since `useMemo` provides no cleanup mechanism (unlike `useEffect`), this timer will fire even if the component unmounts before the timeout completes. Additionally, if React Strict Mode double-invokes the render or dependencies change rapidly, multiple `setTimeout` calls can be created without canceling previous ones, leading to `clearPendingNewTransactionIDs` being called multiple times.

Move the `clearPendingNewTransactionIDs` side effect out of `useMemo` and into a `useEffect` that reacts to the presence of pending new transactions. For example:

```tsx
const newTransactions = useMemo(() => {
    if (transactions === undefined || prevTransactions === undefined || transactions.length <= prevTransactions.length) {
        if (isFocused && reportID && pendingNewTransactionIDs && transactions?.length) {
            const pendingSet = new Set(Object.values(pendingNewTransactionIDs));
            return transactions.filter((transaction) => pendingSet.has(transaction.transactionID));
        }
        return CONST.EMPTY_ARRAY as unknown as Transaction[];
    }
    // ... rest of existing logic
}, [transactions, prevTransactions, pendingNewTransactionIDs, reportID, isFocused]);

// Clear pending IDs in a useEffect with proper cleanup
useEffect(() => {
    if (!newTransactions.length || !reportID || !pendingNewTransactionIDs) {
        return;
    }
    const timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {
        clearPendingNewTransactionIDs(reportID);
    }, CONST.SCREEN_TRANSITION_END_TIMEOUT);
    return () => clearTimeout(timeoutId);
}, [newTransactions, reportID, pendingNewTransactionIDs]);
```

This ensures the timeout is properly cleaned up on unmount and avoids side effects inside `useMemo`.

